this golden retriever--

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.759
friendly, approachable,
helpful, really wanting

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:37.110
to talk to you about open-source
security, so that's my goal.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.190
Right?

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.550
And feel free to
say hi on Twitter

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.250
because I'd love to chat
open-source security with you.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.260
So today, we're going
to do a few things.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.910
First, we're going to do a very
brief overview of serverless

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.820
and how it compares to
some other solutions

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.310
you might have encountered.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:59.340
Then I'm going to show you
a demo app that I built,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.950
and then we're going to hack
it, which is my favorite part.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.580
And finally, I'm going to
give you a few takeaways,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.370
so when you go and build
your serverless app,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:12.320
you have a few things to keep
in mind to help keep it secure.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:12.930
All right.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.260
So when you're working with
a cloud provider like Google

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.660
Cloud Platform, there's
a few different ways

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.090
that you can organize your app.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.810
And there are kind of pros
and cons to both, or to any

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.640
of these, and we'll kind of
do a quick overview of each.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.360
So a monolith is what
I would kind of see

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.670
as a more traditional solution.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:40.560
This is where your team
handles the virtual machines

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.820
and your apps run on that and,
you handle the operating system

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.540
and the security questions
that arise within the operating

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.150
system.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:56.160
So you have to make sure that
you stay patched so you're not

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.020
leaving yourself vulnerable.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.190
And the Cloud Platform very
nicely handles your hardware

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:07.330
and there's just easier
elasticity and host setup.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.090
OK, this is working out.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.960
So with containers,
it's pretty similar.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.630
Your team's going to handle
your virtual machines.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.020
But within those virtual
machines, you have containers,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.190
and those containers can have
different functionality in them

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.710
and each container can have
its own operating system.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.160
So it's just a little
bit more flexible

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.610
and you can spin up a new
container if you need it

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.550
but you don't have to scale
the things you don't need.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.900
And the Cloud platform,
it handles your hardware,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.410
you handle the operating
system, but it's more flexible.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:50.040
And this system is more
portable and it's easier

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.040
elasticity and host setup.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.180
And finally, you
have what we're going

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.520
to talk about today, which
is a serverless setup.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.120
And in this kind of
situation, you only

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.150
really need to worry
about your app, right?

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.260
So you worry about your app
and the functions inside of it,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.490
but the Cloud Platform
handles your virtual machine,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.180
it handles your
operating system,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.323
they handle the hardware.

00:03:19.323 --> 00:03:21.240
You don't have to think
about operating system

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.430
management, your host setup.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.530
This is the best
cloud portability

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.750
and has maximum elasticity.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.890
So this talk is about serverless
security, and you may wonder,

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:37.440
well, I have given a lot
of the security concerns

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.117
over to Google Cloud
Platform and they're doing

00:03:40.117 --> 00:03:41.200
a really good job with it.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.010
So what's left for me to secure?

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.920
And well, I want you to
think about your application.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.490
And this may be a
broader consideration

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:54.300
than you may at
first think, right?

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.450
This little chart here
I have, the yellow

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.340
represents the code
that you wrote.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.240
But the code that you write is
not the entirety of the code

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.510
that you ship.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.790
OK?

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.660
The big blue circle represents
the code that you ship,

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.800
and the part that isn't
the little yellow dot,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.530
well those are
your dependencies.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.660
So let's take an example.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.560
In one of Snyk
serverless demo apps,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.730
we have 222 lines of code.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:24.900
And that's really trim.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:30.840
Not much to survey, and
you could look and find

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.880
security problems pretty quick.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.300
But in addition, we have
five direct dependencies,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.240
and because those dependencies
have dependencies,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.070
we have 54 total dependencies.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.210
And you might shrug at
this but it actually

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.550
adds up to nearly
half a million lines

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.910
of code, which is a lot, right?

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.100
And how many security
vulnerabilities

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.100
are within those half a
million lines of code?

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:58.600
All right.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.420
So Google Cloud
Platform is going

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.620
to secure the operating system
for your serverless app,

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.680
but you need to consider
your application security,

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.640
and you need to consider all
of it, not just the yellow dot.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.425
So let's take a look
at my sample app

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:15.550
and we're going to hack it.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:21.690
All right.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:25.020
Because I like a bit
of whimsy in my life,

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.430
the sample app is a to-do
list obsessively written

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:33.060
by my cat to make sure that
I do all the things that he

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.120
requires.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:34.950
OK?

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:38.670
And I have a function
within this app that renders

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.540
the to-do list really nicely.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.330
So you can see we have the
start of that to-do list here.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:52.380
And if I wanted to add
to this to-do list,

00:05:52.380 --> 00:06:00.540
I can hit the HTTP point
with a simple cURL command.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.520
We already have Feed
Cat on the list,

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.900
so we're going to say Feed
Cat again because that's

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.180
just how it works.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:21.630
And so we see that
an item was created.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:28.510
And when I refresh this, it's
going to show the new task.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:31.155
Right?

00:06:31.155 --> 00:06:33.680
And this is awesome.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.620
Jacques, he's going
to add as many things

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.170
as he wants to, right?

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.605
But maybe I don't
like that so much.

00:06:41.605 --> 00:06:44.650
You know, I'm the boss,
he's not the boss, right?

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:49.870
So what would happen if I
want to take this app down.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.340
To cut it off at its knees.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.760
And I'm going to show
you how we might do that.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:02.590
So first off, I'm going to
show you Snyk's website, where

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.440
I've uploaded this app.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.700
It looks at the
dependencies within the app

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:12.550
and it scans them and it
lets me know when there

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.450
are known vulnerabilities.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.860
So within my dependencies,
there are actually

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.230
nine known vulnerabilities.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:24.970
There may be more but these
are the ones we know about.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.830
They have CDEs, all
those different things.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:32.200
And some of them, we
can exploit and get in.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.020
So the one we're
going to look at

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.730
is a regular expression
denial of service.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:47.050
So this vulnerable module,
MS, it's in our app

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.850
because it helps us parse
time fields, kind of.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.350
So to give you an
example of that,

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.300
let's say my cat really
wanted to take a nap

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.350
but he wanted to be woken up.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:07.680
So he's going to add an
item to the to-do list

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.637
of wake cat in two days.

00:08:09.637 --> 00:08:11.220
So he's going to
take a nice long nap.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.658
And so we do
another cURL command

00:08:18.658 --> 00:08:20.325
and it creates another
item on our list.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.200
And we can see it
here, wake cat,

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.700
and it recognized two
days as a time period

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.799
and so that's why you see
that formatting there.

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:33.299
Right?

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.474
And we could build
functionality around that to,

00:08:35.474 --> 00:08:38.789
like, send out a reminder or
something along those lines,

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:40.870
right?

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:44.280
However, I'm kind of fed
up with this whole thing

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:54.540
and I'm going to try to
create a to-do item that it's

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.280
going to take the system
too long to do matching.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:08:59.940
Right?

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.331
So let's see.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.160
So I'm going to use
this cURL command, which

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:18.290
tries to add a to-do
item to provide snacks

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.540
in an amount of time
that's really long,

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.530
and just to keep it chugging
away and trying to figure out

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:28.940
what's going on a little bit
longer, I misspelled minutes.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.570
OK?

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:31.980
It says here, minutey.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.850
So let's see what happens then.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:40.990
Well, nothing.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:41.650
Right?

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.330
It's still chugging
along, trying

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:46.360
to make a match
somewhere and trying

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:49.300
to do its regular
expression thing, right?

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:53.860
But while it's running
like this, my cat,

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:58.810
whether he wants to or not,
he can't add any new to-dos

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.300
to my to-do app.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.810
And so I really appreciate
that and I'm just really glad

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:10.000
I was able to stop him
in his tracks just using

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.790
this simple regular expression.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:13.960
Right?

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.540
So I'm going to just kill this.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:23.420
So once again, I'm the
master, not Jacques.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:28.310
So let's go back to
the presentation.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.160
So I have a few takeaways
that hopefully you

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.030
can use when you're writing
your next serverless app.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:45.460
And briefly there, number one,
beware of vulnerable libraries.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.730
Number two, deploy granular
functions and permissions.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.850
Number three, don't rely
on function ordering.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:54.590
Number four, don't
rely on immutability.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:59.280
And number five, to worry
about all functions.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.910
So first off, beware of
vulnerable libraries.

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:05.790
Test during development
and monitor over time.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.700
This is exactly
the kind of problem

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:11.510
that I just took
advantage of when I broke

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:14.300
Jacques' to-do list, right?

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.590
And here's a little
snippet of the Snyk website

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.890
and you can see that it'll
list out the vulnerabilities

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.260
and give you a little
report about them.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.980
And so then you know.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:30.600
And it also will tell you
what remediation can be taken.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.100
Right?

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.970
So this is a very important
thing to be aware of.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.660
Secondly, you should
deploy granular functions

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:40.070
and permissions.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.880
It's safer.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:43.940
So think about today.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.520
You were able to come
down into this space

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:48.100
because you have a badge.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:51.350
I'm looking out and you
all have badges on, right?

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:58.220
But your badge doesn't
get you into the entirety

00:11:58.220 --> 00:11:59.480
of this space, right?

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:04.220
There are doors and things that
you need a special badge for

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:09.860
or a different colored badge,
something along those lines.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.700
It would be madness
to issue everyone

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.330
that came to Google Cloud Next
just a generic, like, you're

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.700
allowed everywhere badge, right?

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.260
And it's the same here.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:25.310
Deploy granular functions
and permissions.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:29.610
Don't let everyone
access every function.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.420
It's just better.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:32.990
All right.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:35.810
Don't rely on function ordering.

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:38.690
Every function is a perimeter
that needs to be secured.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.910
And when I think
about this one, I

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:46.910
think about how I don't not
lock my safe just because I've

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:51.410
locked my door, right?

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:54.500
Don't rely on these
initial functions

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.710
to protect the ones that are
maybe further in your app.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:58.250
Right?

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:02.900
Every function has a perimeter
that needs to be secured.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.720
Fourth, don't rely
on immutability.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.690
Assume servers can be reused.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:12.500
And just in general, don't
make assumptions that

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.295
could compromise your security.

00:13:14.295 --> 00:13:16.190
All right?

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.100
And finally, worry
about all functions.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:22.160
Every function increases
your attack surface.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:25.160
Let's say you have a
house that has ten windows

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.830
and you were very
diligent and you

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:33.170
alarmed and locked eight of
them but two of them are open.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.440
Or maybe they're not open
but they're just not locked.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:40.970
Well if you have something
of value in your home,

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.610
the eight highly
secured windows aren't

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:48.530
going to deter someone
who wants to break in

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.780
to get what you have, right?

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:54.080
So it doesn't matter that
you've secured those eight.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:14:00.020
What matters is that there is
a way in in those two windows.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:03.800
So finally, just one
more quick review,

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.670
be aware of
vulnerable libraries,

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.340
deploy granular functions
and permissions,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:12.180
don't rely on function ordering,
don't rely on immutability,

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.570
and worry about all functions.

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.690
Also, don't let your
cat boss you around.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:20.400
So thank you all.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.020
And again, I'm Hayley Denbraver.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.120
I'm a developer
advocate at Snyk.

